# Database connection strings for `npm run migrate` — copy to `.env.db`.
#
#   cp .env.db.example .env.db
#
# `.env.db` is gitignored. These two values are the most dangerous secrets in
# the project: a Postgres URL carries the password for a role that bypasses row
# level security entirely, so a leak is total rather than partial.
#
# Two rules keep them out of the app bundle, and both are worth knowing:
#
#   1. Neither name has an `EXPO_PUBLIC_` prefix. Metro inlines *only* prefixed
#      names, so these are invisible to the bundler. Never add a prefix, and
#      never re-export one of these under a prefixed name "just to read it in
#      the app" — nothing in the app should ever hold a direct database URL.
#   2. They live in `.env.db` rather than `.env`, so the file Expo reads and the
#      file holding the database password are not the same file.
#
# Precedence when the migration runner looks for them (scripts/db-env.mjs):
#   a real environment variable  >  .env.db  >  .env
# So a CI secret or a shell export always wins, and this file is only the
# convenience layer for local work.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Where the values come from
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Supabase → Project Settings → Database → Connection string → URI.
#
# Use the SESSION pooler on port 5432, NOT the transaction pooler on 6543.
# Migrations run DDL inside explicit transactions and hold a session-level
# advisory lock across statements; the transaction pooler supports neither, and
# the failure is confusing rather than clean.
#
#   postgres://postgres.[project-ref]:[password]@aws-0-[region].pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres
#
# STAGING AND PRODUCTION MUST BE TWO DIFFERENT SUPABASE PROJECTS — not two
# schemas in one, and not the same project twice. The whole value of staging is
# that a migration which destroys data destroys nothing that matters, which is
# only true if it is a genuinely separate database. `npm run migrate:reset`
# refuses to run at all when these two resolve to the same host and database
# (scripts/migration-plan.mjs), but that check protects the reset path only.

SUPABASE_DB_URL_STAGING=
SUPABASE_DB_URL_PRODUCTION=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Using them
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   npm run migrate:status         # what is applied where, both environments
#   npm run migrate:staging        # apply everything pending to staging
#   npm run migrate -- --env staging --dry-run
#   npm run migrate:production     # prints the plan, then asks before applying
#   npm run migrate:reset          # staging only: drop public, re-apply from 0001
#
# Always staging first. The runner records a ledger with checksums, so a second
# run is a no-op rather than a pile of errors you have to read carefully.
